#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "07d823d0-39f8-5d70-8df4-7fd15783e148" :question/math-academy-id "e-5427" :question/problem "Are the following transformations linear?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_1.$ Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ (kx)(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ {k}^{2}xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}k{x}^{2} \\\\ kxy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne k{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_1$ is not a linear transformation.\n\nNow, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_2.$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3kx \\\\ kx+2ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(3x) \\\\ k(x+2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}3x \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+2{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/id #uuid "8fe1f455-e739-59cb-95c2-0378dff32b8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5428" :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation and $[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ then find $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]) & =T(6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=6T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "381f7ffe-ae4b-5e45-9fa9-e2a6bd8348b3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "e34b53de-72d8-541f-bd04-16d2cbf1e6b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5429" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ If $[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(4{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =4T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] & =4T({v}_{1})+[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 4T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 4T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "fc3159fb-751c-55d6-b41e-4744b80b0ae9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5430" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ If $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}$ and $[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0},$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} & =5{v}_{1}+12{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}+12({v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}+12(0) \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(5\\mathbf{v}_1).\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(5{v}_{1}) & =5T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =5[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8532f2a-0470-5497-9a97-ae414dd1060d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262565} {:answer/id #uuid "64acbc19-30c2-5c17-9f51-838f600343d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262566} {:answer/id #uuid "70355b54-e5e3-58fa-ab81-fb569f95146a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262567} {:answer/id #uuid "c2bcc7a1-19ed-5439-9594-9c60437fac16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262568} {:answer/id #uuid "17d72357-aae9-5874-a8f6-71a87231f0e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8532f2a-0470-5497-9a97-ae414dd1060d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262565} :answer-field/id #uuid "2033c514-e2c7-5fab-8823-3a450d6ae616" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2b3861b-a381-5b7c-bc56-9232209a631c" :question/math-academy-id "q-12008" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 4kx+3ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed52c73-5e80-5db4-bda4-1dea7f9c2e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303052} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c77d1f-1d58-5fd1-90f0-83c7e53cdc8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303053} {:answer/id #uuid "7d93dd8a-c6d0-5be2-8ae0-58b738a05e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303054} {:answer/id #uuid "694ba66b-7e22-5c5d-bf7f-09db587f68a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303055} {:answer/id #uuid "b16c8d3f-0a3c-5da5-9e42-a52c032def83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ed52c73-5e80-5db4-bda4-1dea7f9c2e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303052} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd05e3bd-8452-5567-b42f-635a28adc8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8a57237-0d4e-5076-9f5f-1a907c4a8585" :question/math-academy-id "q-170589" :question/problem "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]=4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]) & =T(4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])=4T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]) & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8dad40f-0a40-5334-81a8-99d05f057af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-60 \\\\ 24 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303059} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2c8385-8712-513a-b397-433ddc0c0afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-55 \\\\ 36 \\\\ 60\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303060} {:answer/id #uuid "0a23c985-4ec6-55f8-9a47-df543ab4d910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -24 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303061} {:answer/id #uuid "590087a4-576a-5b51-bc56-f83a86d4eae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-60 \\\\ 36 \\\\ 54\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303062} {:answer/id #uuid "17300f69-b781-57d0-bcdc-05f0ff4c943e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-75 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 60\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17300f69-b781-57d0-bcdc-05f0ff4c943e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-75 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 60\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303063} :answer-field/id #uuid "d99d7336-9ccf-53f1-ae68-6e2e086e28e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "449d642a-a86b-521f-8aae-b4adf7bd77fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-170636" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}-25 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find $\\,T(15{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} & =12{v}_{1}+3{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} \\\\  & =12({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+3{v}_{1} \\\\  & =12(0)+3{v}_{1} \\\\  & =3{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(15{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2})=T(3{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}) & =3T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =3[\\begin{aligned}-25 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-75 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 60\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(15{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-75 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 60\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81c82a62-ddc1-55d4-a327-e33f832a42fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262579} {:answer/id #uuid "023228f9-10c2-5966-9c94-62d03a03e90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262580} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfcd828-06b4-5211-a97a-3dfa20875dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262581} {:answer/id #uuid "42a980f6-aae3-5b9f-9317-4532c684a618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262582} {:answer/id #uuid "88e075d6-edf6-53f3-9dd3-0a599c94e17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "023228f9-10c2-5966-9c94-62d03a03e90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262580} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d405b92-6b9d-5e4c-ae79-a33f77221e43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e81b13b4-2381-5cfb-82da-8f1e98b1ff39" :question/math-academy-id "q-170638" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4a33b6a-544a-5387-ba54-82f9bba5ff59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303066} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ce4da4-0c48-5579-9b09-cf4d16e74f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303067} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5a644c-0a2b-5289-8348-5ee4bcb18dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303068} {:answer/id #uuid "61827c17-50a8-5b23-8021-20010be82ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303069} {:answer/id #uuid "1121b90b-2e4c-58b5-9f03-75d06010f56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a5a644c-0a2b-5289-8348-5ee4bcb18dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303068} :answer-field/id #uuid "81bda7b5-fa33-58d6-95af-ed4090bbbee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65a35228-3fa4-5ae7-b7c4-4220ad1c7c6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170647" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ k\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={k}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef581d2-91ff-5334-90a7-ccc5cd8f5b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ef581d2-91ff-5334-90a7-ccc5cd8f5b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303073} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5953d4b-a0b4-5bf0-b100-491623542dd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303072} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d74bf42e-0680-5f4f-85ae-4a2945afb564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d74bf42e-0680-5f4f-85ae-4a2945afb564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303075} :answer-field/id #uuid "065eb33b-8423-5eb5-9bf4-2e08624c7707" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1bb2d9b-c8a5-5a42-a769-fd3bcb07dbb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-317697" :question/problem "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]$, then find $T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]).$\n\n| $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=3[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]) & =T(3[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=3T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f5d7f1-6015-5d1f-ba45-8fabad8d9eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303078} {:answer/id #uuid "6f70bf1b-d486-539d-8b9f-b4e3a80a4e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f5d7f1-6015-5d1f-ba45-8fabad8d9eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303078} :answer-field/id #uuid "