Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "348755b4-5cf8-5edf-bae7-647009b8b908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-42 \\\\ -48 \\\\ -54\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303117} {:answer/id #uuid "09fb761a-36d0-5fcb-8025-50c30b25e0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 54\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303118} {:answer/id #uuid "2e05b932-b200-596b-b727-4a1e1b741fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303119} {:answer/id #uuid "99f23a06-6244-5093-8914-674327114aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -40 \\\\ -45\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303120} {:answer/id #uuid "165cff79-aa46-5d80-b7ea-c38852f0b369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}35 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 45\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99f23a06-6244-5093-8914-674327114aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -40 \\\\ -45\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303120} :answer-field/id #uuid "05299e25-eea1-58ce-9e0c-108550093438" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bde10d1-5d59-51e1-a436-733e237150d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74782" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find $\\,T(11{v}_{1}+6{v}_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11{v}_{1}+6{v}_{2} & =11{v}_{1}+11{v}_{2}-5{v}_{2} \\\\  & =11({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})-5{v}_{2} \\\\  & =11(0)-5{v}_{2} \\\\  & =-5{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(11{v}_{1}+6{v}_{2})=T(-5{v}_{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-5{v}_{2}) & =-5T({v}_{2}) \\\\  & =-5[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -40 \\\\ -45\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(11{v}_{1}+6{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -40 \\\\ -45\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a2275b7-0cd4-5acd-ad72-a48b0b2500ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268006} {:answer/id #uuid "c277f7b3-76e0-5e40-b67a-d1147b5db584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268007} {:answer/id #uuid "62482cca-61a3-5b23-9619-47da3c54bfcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268008} {:answer/id #uuid "28156a0d-ee37-549d-810f-e1b8cedcc9ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268009} {:answer/id #uuid "1f961e99-5b0e-588c-b4b1-413172278b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a2275b7-0cd4-5acd-ad72-a48b0b2500ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268006} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d49dd3-04d8-5449-9d2a-f2ea6067e69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20a14b0f-437e-5229-9343-d166ad27d365" :question/math-academy-id "q-8022" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 2(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ k(2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30236e9b-6cef-5ff8-935d-31ffcb1d64c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303124} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1201de-428e-5cfb-a53e-7f15f4aa2af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303125} {:answer/id #uuid "e653c9e1-3da4-5845-ba53-cd0105a93c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303126} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7098a4-cf8a-556d-8bd3-b993af2a5d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303127} {:answer/id #uuid "a475d07e-edde-51ec-86e0-bfecd24bc2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a1201de-428e-5cfb-a53e-7f15f4aa2af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303125} :answer-field/id #uuid "70cf97b8-ea31-51f0-92e7-4005557b8960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72a3fd90-5133-5a81-a0e8-33ac74b2d520" :question/math-academy-id "q-8049" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."}]}
